[{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Pseudo-Transient-Stokes-Solvers","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"","title":"Pseudo Transient Stokes Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Numerical-Justification","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"The standard incompressible steady-state mass and momentum conservation equations (elliptical) form a saddle point problem with pressure acting as a Lagrangian multiplier. Such systems have the following properties:\n\nIndefinite - The matrix eignevalues are +ve and -ve mixed, with no simple minimum,\nThe system necessitates 'LBB' or 'Inf-Sup' criterion,\nPoor conditioning - Significant difference in magnitudes between velocity and pressure.\n\nThe pseudo-transient approach introduces artificial time derivatives to these conservation equations which embeds regularisation terms into the system. This then provides a pressure block diagonal dominance, suppressing unphysical behaviours and significantly improves the condition number.","title":"Numerical Justification"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Physics-Formulation","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"We begin with the standard steady-state conservation equations for incompressibile and inertialess flow:\n\nConservation of mass,\n\nnablacdotunderlinemathbfv=0\n\nConservation of momentum,\n\n-nabla P + nablacdotunderlinemathbfsigma =\n-rho gunderlinemathbfe_y\n\nWith Newton's consitutive law:\n\n-nabla P + nablacdot(2underlinemathbfdotvarepsiloneta) =\n-rho gunderlinemathbfe_y\n\nIn the pseudo-transient approach, we add artificial time derivatives, partialtau, that recover the original conservation equations as they tend to zero. This transforms the equations from elliptical to parabolic, through a new equation of state (EOS) compressibility term, betafracpartial Ppartialtau, in the conservation of mass, and an inertial term, rho^*fracpartialunderlinemathbfvpartialtau, in the conservation of momentum. Here beta and rho^* are the artifical (non-physical) compressibility and density respectively.\n\nConservation of mass,\n\nbetafracpartial Ppartialtau+nablacdotunderlinemathbfv=0\n\nConservation of momentum,\n\n-nabla P + nablacdot(2underlinemathbfdotvarepsiloneta) -\nrho^*fracpartialunderlinemathbfvpartialtau =\n-rho gunderlinemathbfe_y\n\nAssuming incompressibility, underlinemathbfdotvarepsilon = underlinemathbfdotvarepsilon,\n\n-nabla P + nablacdot((nablamathbfv + (nablamathbfv)^T)eta) -\nrho^*fracpartialunderlinemathbfvpartialtau =\n-rho gunderlinemathbfe_y\n\nFor constant eta,\n\nfracpartialunderlinemathbfvpartialtau = frac1rho^*(\n-nabla P + etanabla^2mathbfv + etanabla(nablacdotmathbfv)\n+rho gunderlinemathbfe_y)\n\nWe may then derive hyperbolic equations which are governed by the CFL stability condition, such that the pseudo-time step (Deltatau) is directly proportional to the spatial resolution (Delta x). This is advantageous as a typical parabolic systems has Delta t propto (Delta x)^2, meaning a doubling in the resolution requires quartering the time step. This hyperbolic equation can be found by taking the pseudo-time derivative of the continuity equation and substituting the conservation of momentum equation,\n\nfracpartialpartialtau(Conservation of mass),\n\nfracpartial^2 Ppartialtau^2 = -frac1betanablacdot(fracpartial underlinemathbfvpartialtau)\n\nSubstitute conservation of momentum,\n\nfracpartial^2 Ppartialtau^2 = -frac1betanablacdot(frac1rho^*(\n-nabla P + etanabla^2mathbfv + etanabla(nablacdotmathbfv)\n+rho gunderlinemathbfe_y)\n)\n\nExpanding,\n\nfracpartial^2 Ppartialtau^2 = underbracefrac1betarho^*nabla^2P_textClassic Wave Equation\n- underbracefrac1betarho^*nablacdot(rho gunderlinemathbfe_y)_textBuoyancy Source - underbracefracetabetarho^*\n    2nabla^2(nablacdotmathbfv)_textViscous Damping\n\n\nSubstituting compressibility,\n\nfracpartial^2 Ppartialtau^2 = underbracefrac1betarho^*nabla^2P_textClassic Wave Equation\n- underbracefrac1betarho^*nablacdot(rho gunderlinemathbfe_y)_textBuoyancy Source - underbracefracetabetarho^*\n    2nabla^2(-betafracpartial Ppartialtau)_textViscous Damping\n\n\nDamped Wave Equation,\n\nfracpartial^2 Ppartialtau^2 = underbracefrac1betarho^*nabla^2P_textClassic Wave Equation\n+underbracefracetarho^*\n    2nabla^2(fracpartial Ppartialtau)_textViscous Damping\n\n\nBy combining these conservation equations we form a hyperbolic equation, with a characteristic wave speed of c^2 = frac1betacdotrho^*.","title":"Physics Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Dispersion-Analysis","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"A critical feature of the pseudo-transient method is finding terms for beta, rho^* and Deltatau, which both stabilises the system and provides rapid numerical convergence.\n\nAnalysing the system, we may substitute a Fourier ansatz, P(underlinemathbfxtau) = hatP(tau)e^iunderlinemathbfkcdotunderlinemathbfx, into the damped wave equation,\n\nhatP(tau) + frac2etaunderlinemathbfk^2rho^*hatP(tau) +\nfracunderlinemathbfk^2betarho^*hatP(tau) = 0\n\nfor underlinemathbfk = k_xunderlinemathbfi + k_y underlinemathbfj. By substituting hatP(tau)=e^gammatau into the linear homogeneous ordinary differential equation, we find root solutions of the form\n\ngamma = -fracetaunderlinemathbfk^2rho^* pm sqrtfraceta^2underlinemathbfk^4rho^*2-fracunderlinemathbfk^2betarho^*\n\nwhere we seek the minimum real root solutions the exponential decay rate, gamma. In the first case, critical dampening may be obtained when the discriminant, D, is zero,\n\nD = fraceta^2underlinemathbfk^4rho^*2-fracunderlinemathbfk^2betarho^* = 0\n\ntherefore fraceta^2underlinemathbfk^2rho^*2 = c^2\n\nsubstituting the wavespeed relation c^2 = frac1betacdotrho^*. If D0, then we obtain two roots for gamma,\n\ngamma_1 = -fracetaunderlinemathbfk^2rho^* + sqrtD\n\ngamma_2 = -fracetaunderlinemathbfk^2rho^* - sqrtD\n\nFor D  0, both roots are negative and gamma_2  gamma_1. The gamma_2 mode decays faster, but a general solution contains both modes, whereby its long-time convergence is governed by the slower-decaying gamma_1 mode.","title":"Dispersion Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Parameter-Estimation","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"In the numerical setup, we seek parameters which will attenuate a given wave over the domain length, L_c, for L_c =operatornamemax(Width Height).\n\nAs the numerical scheme below combines explicit with semi-implicit formulations, the pseudo-timestep, Deltatau, is limited by the CFL condition,\n\ncDeltatau = operatornameCFLfracDelta hsqrtn\n\nwhere n is the number of spatial dimensions. As such, the code choses a fixed arbitrary c and then derives Deltatau from the CFL condition,\n\nDeltatau = operatornameCFLfracDelta hcsqrtn\n\nFrom the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem, lambda_min = 2Delta h, we can relate the characteristic wavenumber, k_c, to the characteristic gridspacing, Delta h by,\n\nunderlinemathbfk = k_c=frac2pilambda = fracpiDelta h\n\nFollowing the critical dampening equation,\n\nfraceta^2 underlinemathbfk^2rho^*2 = fraceta^2pi^2rho^*2Delta h^2 = c^2\n\nWhich can be reduced when all parameters are positive to,\n\nrho^* = fracetapicDelta h\n\nand\n\nbeta = frac1rho^*c^2","title":"Parameter Estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Discretised-Updates-and-Convergence","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"The explicit velocity update from the modified momentum equation is\n\nunderlinemathbfv^l+1 = underlinemathbfv^l + fracDeltataurho^*left(\n-nabla P^l + etanabla^2underlinemathbfv^l - underlinemathbffright)\n\nfor the current pseudo-transient iteration, l. The pressure update from the modified continuity equation then uses the updated velocities from the momentum equation via\n\nP^l+1 = P^l - fracDeltataubetanablacdotunderlinemathbfv^l+1\n\nThe pseudo-transient approach drives solutions to the steady-state as the pseudo timesteps tend to zero. As such, convergence is evaluated by the change in parameters across a pseudo timestep, made relative to the magnitude of the overall driving forcing f_max. These residuals are determined by,\n\nr_velocity = fracleftunderlinemathbfv^l+1-underlinemathbfv^lright_inftyrho^*Deltatau f_maxqquad\nr_pressure = fracleftP^l+1-P^lright_inftybetaDeltatau f_maxDelta h\n\nwhere an arbitrary tolerance, max(10^-6 1000epsilon), is pre-set given a machine epsilon, epsilon, of the floating point number of the system, relating the differences in precision between Float32 and Float64. The solver further provides a warning if the maximum number of iterations is reached without convergence.\n\nSee Alkhimenkov and Podladchikov (2025) for the accelerated pseudo-transient method.","title":"Discretised Updates and Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Model-Setup","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"Using the pseudo transient approach, the staggered-finite-difference formulation from the monolithic approach is modified to eliminate ghost nodes, and the requirements for global indexing are relaxed. As such, the array sizes for Vx, Vy, and P are not equal, and additional boundary vectors are deployed (see Boundary Conditions).\n\n(Image: 2D SFD Grid Layout)","title":"Model Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#Boundary-Conditions","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"The implemented staggered-finite-difference scheme affords collated boundary nodes on Vx and Vy, but not for all boundaries. As such, from boundary condition functions, the scheme implements the respective boundary conditions implicitly.\n\nX-Stokes boundary condition layout (Image: X-Stokes Boundary Condition Layout)","title":"Boundary Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"psuedotransient/#References","page":"Psuedo Transient Stokes Solvers","text":"Alkhimenkov, Y. and Podladchikov, Y.Y., 2025. Accelerated pseudo-transient method for elastic, viscoelastic, and coupled hydromechanical problems with applications. Geoscientific Model Development, 18(2), pp.563-583.","title":"References"},{"category":"type","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.BCConfig","page":"Solver Core","text":"BCConfig{BC1, BC2, BC3}\n\nStructure containing the boundary condition structures for each variable vx, vy and pressure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.BCConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.BCs2D","page":"Solver Core","text":"BCs2D{L, R, T, B}\n\nStructure containing the boundary conditions for the 2D domain. Can be single values or functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.BCs2D"},{"category":"type","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.Coords","page":"Solver Core","text":"Coords{XVEC, YVEC}\n\nBasic structure containing x and y vector coordinates\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.Coords"},{"category":"type","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.PerformanceData","page":"Solver Core","text":"PerformanceData\n\nStruct to pass solver performance outputs to the Benchmarks module for  evaluation and comparisons.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.PerformanceData"},{"category":"type","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.VarCoords","page":"Solver Core","text":"VarCoords{VX, VY, P}\n\nStructure of structures containing the x and y coordinates for variables vx, vy and pressure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.VarCoords"},{"category":"method","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.coord_allocation-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Solver Core","text":"coord_allocation(dx,dy,xsize,ysize)\n\nStruct to store coordinate vectors for x-y Stokes and Continuity equation nodal points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.coord_allocation"},{"category":"method","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.coord_allocation_direct-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Solver Core","text":"coord_allocation_direct(dx,dy,xsize,ysize)\n\nStruct to store coordinate vectors for x-y Stokes and Continuity equation nodal points using the DirectMonolithis approach\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.coord_allocation_direct"},{"category":"method","location":"solvercore/#RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.eval_forcing!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Solver Core","text":"eval_forcing!(fy, rho, gy)\n\nFunction to unpack density array into forcing term in y-Stokes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.SolverCore.eval_forcing!"},{"category":"section","location":"#RelledomMultiPhysics.jl","page":"Home","text":"Welcome!","title":"RelledomMultiPhysics.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"Description here!","title":"Overview"}]
